<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayezid I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1389">1389</a>–<a href="/years/1402">1402</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Bayezid I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bayezid I was an Ottoman sultan of the late fourteenth century, known for rapid conquests in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. He was defeated and captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ankara">Battle of Ankara</a> in 1402. The defeat temporarily destroyed Ottoman military power and triggered a long succession crisis, an episode central to the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayezid-i.jpg" alt="Bayezid I" width="314" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1460 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_accession_of_Sultan_Bayezid_I_(enthroned)._Ahmed%C3%AE,_Iskendername,_VBNM,_circa_1460.,_Cod._Or._XC_(57),_y._240b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bayezid was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> and his Greek wife Gülçiçek Hatun. His first major role was as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Kütahya</a>, which he gained by marrying the daughter of a Germiyanid ruler. An impetuous soldier, he earned the nickname Thunderbolt (Yıldırım) in a battle against the Karamanids. He came to the throne after his father was killed by the Serbian knight Miloš Obilić during or just after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1389. Soon afterwards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and Bayezid had his younger brother strangled to avoid a plot. In 1390 he married Olivera Despina, daughter of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar of Serbia</a>, and recognised Lazar's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-lazarevic">Stefan Lazarević</a> as the new Serbian leader.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In Anatolia Bayezid first secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a> from Islamic scholars to justify wars against Muslim states, since he needed the gazis as warriors. In a single campaign in 1390 he conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a> of Aydin, Saruhan and Menteshe, and then overwhelmed Hamid, Teke and Germiyan and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a>. He made peace with Karaman in 1391, then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kastamonu">Kastamonu</a> and Sinop before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadi-burhan-al-din">Kadi Burhan al-Din</a> stopped him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kirkdilim">Battle of Kırkdilim</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> in 1391, and from 1389 to 1395 conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. He crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> against Wallachia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mircea-the-elder">Mircea the Elder</a> but was stopped at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rovine">Battle of Rovine</a>.</p><p>He adopted the title Sultan-i Rûm and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1394, and again from 1395 to 1402. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> organised after appeals by the Byzantine emperor Manuel II Palaeologus, led by Sigismund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nicopolis">Battle of Nicopolis</a> in 1396; Bayezid built the Ulu Cami in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a> to celebrate. At this time his realm included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> except Constantinople, Macedonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>. In 1397 he killed the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a> at Akçay and annexed his territory, and in 1398 he conquered the Djanik emirate and Burhan al-Din's lands, violating an accord with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> roused the Turkic beyliks that had been Ottoman vassals to attack Bayezid. Bayezid lifted his siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and on 20 July 1402 the Ottoman army was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ankara">Battle of Ankara</a>. Bayezid was captured and died in captivity on 8 March 1403.</p><p>A European legend held that he was kept in a cage and humiliated, with his wife serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> at dinner. Early accounts, among them the traveller Johann Schiltberger and Timur's court historian Sharafaddin Yazdi, mention no cage and report respectful treatment. The cage first appears in later writers such as the Ottoman historian Mehmed Neshri. Four of his sons escaped the battlefield and fought the civil war known as the Ottoman Interregnum, which ended with the coronation of Mehmed I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bayezid I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Battle of the Maritsa in 1371, Ivan Shishman of Tarnovo became a vassal of Sultan Murad I. <b>Bayezid I</b> captured Tarnovo on 17 July 1393 after a three-month siege and sent Patriarch Euthymius into exile; Ivan Shishman died in 1395, probably executed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Ankara in 1402 he defeated the Ottoman <b>Sultan Bayezid I</b>, who was captured and died in captivity the following year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Ottoman Interregnum, Süleyman, a son of <b>Bayezid I</b>, was captured and strangled after his army defected to his brother Musa Çelebi at Edirne.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-17">17 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Ottoman Interregnum, the war among the sons of <b>Bayezid I</b>, Süleyman Çelebi defeated his brother Musa Çelebi outside the Ottoman capital, Edirne.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-11">11 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On the Çubuk plain near Ankara, Timur won a crushing victory over the Ottoman sultan <b>Bayezid I</b>, who was captured and died in captivity.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-28">28 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The last battle of the Ottoman Interregnum pitted two sons of <b>Bayezid I</b>, Mehmed Çelebi and Musa Çelebi; Musa was captured and executed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-5">5 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksehir">Akşehir</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.67,26.57&amp;year=1395" aria-label="Edirne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5197 679 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bayezid I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5647 949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5647 949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.67,26.57&amp;year=1395">What was here in 1395? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Thy armies are innumerable: be they so; but what are the arrows of the flying Tartar against the cimeters and battle-axes of my firm and invincible Janizaries?&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Timur, c. 1400 (in Gibbon, Decline and Fall, chapter 65) &#183; tr. Edward Gibbon, 1788 &#183; as Gibbon gives it; two years later Timur defeated and captured him at Ankara &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/736/pg736-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;By the divine clemency, our invincible cimeter has reduced to our obedience almost all Asia, with many and large countries in Europe, excepting only the city of Constantinople; for beyond the walls thou hast nothing left.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the emperor Manuel II (in Gibbon, Decline and Fall, chapter 64) &#183; tr. Edward Gibbon, 1788 &#183; as Gibbon gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/736/pg736-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
